  "articleBody": "Getting a residential roof condition assessment in Texas is a smart move, especially given the intense weather our roofs endure. Whether you’re preparing to buy or sell a home, or just concerned after a severe storm, knowing what an assessment entails can help you feel more confident about the process. It's designed to give you a clear picture of your roof's current health.\n\nThe assessment typically starts with establishing the scope, often reviewing any available roof age, repair, or leak history you might have. This helps the inspector understand your roof's background before they even step on the property. Then, the real inspection begins, usually from multiple vantage points to ensure thoroughness.\n\nExpect the inspector to examine your roof from the ground, the eaves, and safely from the roof surface itself where possible. They'll meticulously check the roof-covering materials, gutters, downspouts, vents, flashing, skylights, chimneys, and all penetrations. A key part of this is looking for evidence of active or potential leaks, so they'll also check accessible attic areas for any signs of water intrusion or structural issues.\n\nThey'll also pay close attention to drainage to ensure water is flowing correctly off your roof. Throughout the process, the inspector should be documenting their findings, often with photographs. Afterward, you’ll receive a written report detailing any defects found, clearly noting any limitations (like areas that couldn't be accessed), and providing recommended next steps. This report is your guide to understanding what your roof needs.\n\nWhat to Expect During the Process:\nDuring a residential roof condition assessment with Discounted Roofing Co., our team focuses on transparent communication and thoroughness. We'll walk you through our findings and answer all your questions, providing a clear roadmap for your roof's future. Our goal is to give you peace of mind.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-02"
}